# Architecture Rules

## Layering (strict, top to bottom)
UI (HTL/JS) -> Servlet (REST) -> Service (business logic) -> Repository (interface) -> CF Adapter -> Content Fragments

## Repository Pattern (CRITICAL for swappability)
- ALWAYS define an interface (Port) first, then a Content Fragment implementation (Adapter)
- Each repository impl is an OSGi @Component with property "impl.type=contentfragment"
- Services reference repositories via @Reference(target="(impl.type=contentfragment)")
- Future DB adapters will use "impl.type=database" — UI/Service/Servlet MUST NOT change to switch

## DTOs
- All data crossing the repository boundary is a plain POJO DTO
- NEVER leak ContentFragment, Resource, ResourceResolver, or JCR types above the repository layer
- DTOs live in com.mysite.core.dto (TicketDTO, CommentDTO, UserDTO)

## Relationships
- Store relationships as text-ID fields (foreign keys), NOT AEM Content References
  (e.g., Comment.ticketId is a String; Ticket.assignedTo is a userId String)
- Keeps a clean migration path to a relational DB

## Responsibilities
- Servlets: thin — parse request, call service, serialize response, set HTTP status. No business logic.
- Services: business logic, validation, state-machine enforcement, orchestration
- Repositories: persistence only (CRUD + queries), no business rules

## Package Structure (under com.mysite.core)
- .dto
- .repositories            (interfaces)
- .repositories.impl       (CF adapters)
- .services                (interfaces)
- .services.impl
- .servlets
- .models                  (Sling Models)
- .statemachine
- .util
- .exception               (domain exceptions)
